<[jack woehr] JAX> from Berkeley fing
<[jack woehr] JAX> oops for Berkeley FIG, shoud hav e been
<[jack woehr] JAX> ga
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ok
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> I saw him at FORML, and he said...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> he only had "about 20-30 bugs left" in V3...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> he was going to do some testing here....
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> but I just wondered if anyone had heard/seen him lately.
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> anyway, tell us more about the 8 jack...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> what type of I/O do you get?
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ga
<[jack woehr] JAX> ok
<[jack woehr] JAX> f0rty i/o pins grouped in 5 programmable 8 bit ports
<[jack woehr] JAX> uart on board the chip
<[jack woehr] JAX> ( full duplex)
<[jack woehr] JAX> 325 regs
<[jack woehr] JAX> 27 interrupt sources
<[jack woehr] JAX> 16 interrupt vectors
<[jack woehr] JAX> 8 interrupt levels
<[jack woehr] JAX> $4.95 a piece in lots of a hundred!
<[jack woehr] JAX> that's what jax calls I/O :-)
<[jack woehr] JAX> ga
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ok
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> yeh!
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> how many pins is the package...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> and what do you do with 325 regs...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> or is that your stack?
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ga
<[jack woehr] JAX>
<[jack woehr] JAX> actually, if you want, you can split the 256 lower regs ...
<[jack woehr] JAX> into four return stacks for four tasks ...
<[jack woehr] JAX> 48 pin or 68 pin
<[jack woehr] JAX> can address 64 k internal memory
<[jack woehr] JAX> and 64 k additional external ;mem
<[jack woehr] JAX> you guessed it ... S8 forth is designed around internal
<[jack woehr] JAX> but you can get at external if you want to bad enough.
<[jack woehr] JAX> one feature of the super8 that Forth locks you out of, 
<[jack woehr] JAX> though ...
<[jack woehr] JAX> the hardware IP is utilised in a conflicting fashion by the 
<[jack woehr] JAX> " fast interrupt"
<[jack woehr] JAX> system, as a pointer to the vector for the fast interrupt ,,,
<[jack woehr] JAX> ss o this feature is not available from FORTH, jaxthinx, as 
<[jack woehr] JAX> he understands
<[jack woehr] JAX> the literchoor.
<[jack woehr] JAX> ga
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> hum
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> what cind of "work around" could we think of?
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> the IP is a pointer to the int vector?
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> so you have to leave it pointed to...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> vector tables all the time...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> or when you expect an interupt?
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ga
<[jack woehr] JAX>
<[jack woehr] JAX> This conflict has nothing to do with ordinary interrupts ...
<[jack woehr] JAX> It is just that ..
<[jack woehr] JAX> Zilog offers in the Super 8 a special hi-speed interrupt 
<[jack woehr] JAX> that takes almost
<[jack woehr] JAX> 1/2 the clocks of normal int processing ...
<[jack woehr] JAX> it is called, appropriately enough, a " fast interrupt"
<[jack woehr] JAX> and the Super8 Manual (c) 198 Zilog says on page 3-7
<[jack woehr] JAX> " The IP can also be used in the fast interrupt processing 
<[jack woehr] JAX> mode ... It can ..
<[jack woehr] JAX> either be use for interrupt processing or imaginary machine 
<[jack woehr] JAX> processing ( FORTH)
<[jack woehr] JAX> _but not for both at the same time_ "
<[jack woehr] JAX> ga
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ok
<PSNIVELY> is here.
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> how is this "fast int" triggered on the S8?
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ga
<[jack woehr] JAX> " Any one of the eight interrupt levels ...
<[jack woehr] JAX> " can be programmed to use ( fast interrupts) by loading the 
<[jack woehr] JAX> fast interrupt ...
<[jack woehr] JAX> select filed of the System Mode register ( R222) with the 
<[jack woehr] JAX> level number ...
<[jack woehr] JAX> and setting the fast interrupt enable bit." s8 manual
<[jack woehr] JAX> ga
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ok
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> welcome Paul, Jack is giving us...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> an excelent tutorial on the Super 8...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> and we are trying to work out...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> how to use the "fast interrupt"...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> it sounds like that could be used...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> Pardon my ignorance, but what's the Super 8?
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> as a fast vectored executer...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> The Super 8 is a ZILOG processor...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> right Jack?
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ga
<[jack woehr] JAX> Correct, Dennis ... from the people who brought you the Z80.
<[jack woehr] JAX> You could always push the IP somewhere & execute code during 
<[jack woehr] JAX> an interrupt
<[jack woehr] JAX> service interval, but you would have to restore it before 
<[jack woehr] JAX> executing NEXT
<[jack woehr] JAX> since the inner interpreter is designed around these fixed 
<[jack woehr] JAX> machine instructions,
<[jack woehr] JAX> and not around a software emulation of them.
<[jack woehr] JAX> ga
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ga paul
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> Doesn't the whole "inner interpreter" concept go bye-bye 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> on a FORTH processor?
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> ga
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ok
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> not really on this...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> at least it sounds like...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> the S8 is maintainting the IP in hardware...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> I don't think it really is like...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> a "classical" Forth engine....]
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> but I think jack could explain that better.
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> can you shed any light jack?
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ga
<M.HAWLEY> is here.
<[jack woehr] JAX> The super8 has been a real eye opener, dennis ...
<[jack woehr] JAX> It actually is pretty much a "classical" Forth engine...
<[jack woehr] JAX> Whereas the Novix is sort of an innovation by comparison ...
<[jack woehr] JAX> The fertile mind of Cowboy didn't go to sleep in 1972 when 
<[jack woehr] JAX> he invented Forth ...
<[jack woehr] JAX> and as he himself says ...
<[jack woehr] JAX> " forth is to me and approach more than a language"
<[jack woehr] JAX> but the Super8 implementation is better described ...
<[jack woehr] JAX> in the Zen-like words of the Manual on page 3-7:
<[jack woehr] JAX> " A threaded-code langauge may be considered ...
<[jack woehr] JAX> " to have created a higher level imaginary machine ...
<[jack woehr] JAX> " within the actual hardware machine ..."[A
<[jack woehr] JAX> " for comparison purpose, the IP is to the imaginary machine 
<[jack woehr] JAX> " as the Program Counter is to the actual hardware machine"
<[jack woehr] JAX> FORTH is a dream, jaxthinx ... something we approach 
<[jack woehr] JAX> parabolically ...
<[jack woehr] JAX> The Super 8 is one version of that dream, the novix is 
<[jack woehr] JAX> another, perhaps
<[jack woehr] JAX> better, but ...
<[jack woehr] JAX> THE SUPER 8 IS CHEAP AND EASY TO PROGRAM
<[jack woehr] JAX> !!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!! :-)
<[jack woehr] JAX> ga
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ok
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> I think that clears that up :-)
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ga paul, sorry, almost forgot
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> S'ok!  Jack, thanks for the insights...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> It's occurred to me many times that, philosophically, 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> FORTH and Lisp are...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> very similar (and I happen to love them both).
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> Both encourage abstraction that can best be described as 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> "metalinguistic."
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> That is to say, Lisp and FORTH are not languages that 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> you write programs in...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> They are languages that you write languages in.
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> I find them both very flexible and powerful, albeit in 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> some cases for different reasons.
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> BTW, I got MacFORTH Plus K3.53 recently... VERY nice!  
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> VERY Lispish in terms of its user interface and even
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> with stuff like Phrases and Relative Chains.
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> ga
<[jack woehr] JAX>
<[jack woehr] JAX> agree they are langs you write langs in.
<[jack woehr] JAX> interesting to note ...
<[jack woehr] JAX> that LISP was a big "underground" hit ...
<[jack woehr] JAX> when Cowboy was a young programmer ... er, oops, youngER 
<[jack woehr] JAX> programmer :-)
<[jack woehr] JAX> ga
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ok
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> does that mean we have another ten years...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> to go before people think we're "great" too?
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ga paul
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> *GRIN*  Could be, Dennis...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> An interesting thing to note about Lisp is that it 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> encourages good program development by providing
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> LOTS of tools...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> Source-level debugging, tracing, definition tracking...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> The MIT hackers created all of this stuff YEARS ago.
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> Problem was, you needed a great CPU and LOTS of RAM/disk 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> space to use it!
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> FORTH evolved from being a very small, simple kernel...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> To being something that can also support source-level 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> debugging, tracing, definition tracking (LOCATE)...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> As the hardware to support that became more widely 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> available.
<M.HAWLEY> has left.
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> Again, it amazes me how much MacFORTH Plus resembles the 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> Lisp system that I use (from a tool/functional
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> standpoint).
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> ga
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ok
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> yes, I think Don Colburn has seen what is needed...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> on the Mac, that stuff has been possible...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> it is starting to be usable on the PC...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> but, still not many other Forth vendors...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> have seen the need to provide this...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> integrated "tool kit"....
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> Forth always has been "minimal"...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> and the transition to "maximal" will be difficult...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> however, I'm looking forward to it...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> I've seen the environments that can be had...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> Haven't seen Mac Forth, but the stuff that...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> Microsoft has created for C is pretty impressive...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> should we/can we compete with that stuff?
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ga paul
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> Sure we can/should, Dennis...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> MacFORTH Plus goes a LONG way towards doing so!
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> And I agree re: environments: why should Smalltalk and 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> Lisp have all the good ones?
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> We have some POWERFUL building blocks...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> The revolution started once we got away from the "FORTH 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> as OS" mentality that kept us out of our machine's
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> native operating systems...
<[jack woehr] JAX> boo hiss!
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> Once we had real OS support, we found that we could DO 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> things to interact with our encironment better...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> Er, environment...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> Jack, raise your hackles, did I?
<[jack woehr] JAX> woof!
<[jack woehr] JAX> Standalone is the Mainstream.
<[jack woehr] JAX> ga
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> Still and all...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> Jack, that's nonsense.  For FORTH it's history, but 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> FORTH can be much more than an OS replacement.
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> And SHOULD be, in my opinion.
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> For one thing, you can't "replace" an OS that's in ROM 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> (Macintosh).
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> In the Macintosh's case, you don't want to anyway.
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> I LIKE being able to write FORTH programs that interact 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> well with non-FORTH programs!
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> I LIKE having a REAL text editor!
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> I LIKE being able to do what I could do in C or Pascal, 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> only better/faster!
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> ga
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> *ok
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> yes paul, so do I, but...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> there is the "other" side...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> and jack makes a valid point for them...
<[jack woehr] JAX> will make!
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> but Forth can be both...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> I agree. guys... but for too long that's ALL FORTH was!
<[Gary] GARY-S> !!
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ga gary, go at it!
<[Gary] GARY-S> Please note - there is also a return to minimal systems...
<[Gary] GARY-S> re - Martin Tracy's Zen Forth. So there..
<[Gary] GARY-S> really is both ends of a spectrum...
<[Gary] GARY-S> only coverable in Forth.
<[Gary] GARY-S> ga
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ga paul
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> I agree and understand, but allow me to state my 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> preference for large extensible systems with LOTS
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> of tools (e.g. Smalltalk) to minimalist systems.
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> It IS nice to start with a basic kernel, then add tools 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> as source code as you see fit... then snapshot...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> Which is PRECISELY what I've done with my MacFORTH 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> system (started as 64K, snapshot is about 200K).
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> And then, when you turnkey your program... out goes the 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> unused code... and you have a 100K application or
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> whatever..
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> I like being able to CHOOSE my large, high-powered 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> environment.  FORTH offers that, but also offers the
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> minimal to those who want it.
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> ga
<[Gary] GARY-S> I have to agree...
<[Gary] GARY-S> the zip in meta compilers are great...
<[Gary] GARY-S> just pointing out there are different strokes.
<[Gary] GARY-S> ga
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ga paul
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> LSure and begorrah, Gary and Jack!  Never meant to imply 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> otherwise!
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> ga
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ok
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> I think there will be more of this...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> contention between the sides...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> Forth is GREAT as a minimal system!
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> but it is also GREAT in a large environment...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> in the large system however...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> it is just another one of the many tools...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> we have in out tool kit...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> which also means we don't just use Forth...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> which is not true in the "minimal" system...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> In other words...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> the people who like it big...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> want it to work with/like the rest of the world...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> but minimalists like it as tradtional Forth...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ie block files, and Forth as the OS.
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ga paul
<[Gary] GARY-S> !!
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> Where was I...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> Oh, yes.  I definitely DON'T use "just FORTH."  I'm 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> polylingual...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> I use whatever I think fits the job...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> But "minimal FORTH" is just too isolated-feeling for me..
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> When I was working on the TRS-80, I had a FORTH that was 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> non-TRS-DOS based... and therefore was basically
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> useless to me (for what I did).
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> It was also doggy slow!  The Z-80 SUCKS as a FORTH 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> engine (no 16-bit indirect relative addressing mode,
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> one hardware stack).
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> Anyway, I like FORTH's flexibility (again, like Lisp)...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> BTW, in the Lisp world, the same conflict exists (Scheme 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> is minimal, Common Lisp is maximal).
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> ga
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ga gary
<[Gary] GARY-S> Make no mistake about this - We as a community are going to 
<[Gary] GARY-S> have to address the...
<[Gary] GARY-S> of the engineer and/or user who wants...
<[Gary] GARY-S> tools no a job of programming, so Paul is dead right...
<[Gary] GARY-S> in that respect. There  will also always be the program hacker.
<[Gary] GARY-S> ga
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ok
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> I think we have to address...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> the "just another tool" philosophy...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> if we are going to gain more user acceptance...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> it is very hard to commit totally to Forth....
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> I'd go so far as to call it impossible.
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> and requires more "thought" than if it is just there...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> as another tool, well then it is easier...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> you can "sneak it in" when the need is there...
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> and then the "needs" WILL grow.
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ga paul
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> OK...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> Um, has anyone other than me noticed the most important 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> thing (to me, anyway)...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> About both Lisp and FORTH?
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> I'll tell you what I think it is...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> Both are environments which encourage programming as an 
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> extension of the language...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> That is, take a look at a Lisp program, like Notecards.  
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> It runs on a Lisp machine.  It's extensible...
<[Paul@ICOM] PSNIVELY> you add to it by writing more Lisp.
